---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import LessonHeader from '../../components/LessonHeader.astro';
import LessonMeta from '../../components/LessonMeta.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const nav = [['Required · Start','#start-here'],['Required · Foundation','#foundation'],['Required · Targeting','#lesson-1'],['Optional · xAPI','#lesson-2'],['Optional · CSV','#lesson-3'],['Advanced · Proxy','#lesson-4'],['Optional · Macros','#lesson-5'],['Recovery','#recovery'],['Capstone','#capstone']];
const lessons = [['01','Choose the right targets','Required','Beginner · 15 min · No change','#lesson-1'],['02','Run xAPI and capture output','Optional','Intermediate · 20 min · Controlled change','#lesson-2'],['03','Make CSV work reliably','Optional','Intermediate · 25 min · Pre-flight first','#lesson-3'],['04','Use a TLS inspection proxy','Advanced','Skip unless your network uses a proxy or TLS inspection','#lesson-4'],['05','Deploy and remove a macro','Optional','Intermediate · 25 min · Reversible change','#lesson-5']];
---
<BaseLayout title="Field Course — CE-Deploy" description="Practical CE-Deploy training with guided exercises, checks, and recovery steps.">
  <div class="page-hero training-hero"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">CE-Deploy field course</p><h1>Learn the workflow.<br/>Prove every result.</h1><p>Start with setup and the foundation. Then use one pilot device to practice targeting, xAPI, CSV, proxy troubleshooting, and macro deployment.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="#start-here">Start the course</a><a class="btn" href="#lessons">View lessons</a></div><div class="course-warning"><strong>Course safety rule:</strong> use a lab organization or one representative pilot device. Read every Pre-flight result before continuing.</div></div></div>
  <nav class="course-nav" aria-label="Course navigation"><div class="container course-nav-inner">{nav.map(([label,href]) => <a href={href}>{label}</a>)}</div></nav><div class="progress-bar"><div class="container progress-inner"><span><b id="progressCount">0 of 6</b> required and guided activities complete</span><button type="button" id="resetProgress">Reset progress</button></div></div>

  <section id="start-here" class="start-section"><div class="container"><header class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Start here</p><h2>Install, verify, and choose your organization type.</h2><p>Complete these tasks before Lesson 1.</p></header><div class="start-grid">
    <article class="setup-card"><span class="setup-number">1</span><h3>Install the current release</h3><p>The buttons open the latest GitHub release. Under <b>Assets</b>, choose the file described below.</p><div class="platform"><div><b>macOS</b><span>macOS 12+ · Apple silicon and Intel</span><small>Choose the Universal <code>.dmg</code> for a normal install. Use the <code>.zip</code> when you need the unpacked application.</small></div><a class="btn primary" href="https://github.com/voipnorm/CE-Deploy/releases/latest">Open macOS assets ↗</a></div><div class="platform"><div><b>Windows</b><span>64-bit Windows 10+</span><small>Choose the installer <code>.exe</code> for a normal install or the portable <code>.exe</code> when installation is restricted.</small></div><a class="btn primary" href="https://github.com/voipnorm/CE-Deploy/releases/latest">Open Windows assets ↗</a></div><p class="minor"><a href={`${base}downloads/`}>Compare formats and requirements →</a></p><div class="checkpoint"><b>Version check:</b> select <b>Settings → About</b>. Confirm the displayed version matches the release you intended to install.</div></article>
    <article class="setup-card"><span class="setup-number">2</span><h3>Choose the correct course path</h3><div class="path-grid"><div><b>Webex Commercial</b><p>Choose <b>Cloud — Webex Control Hub (Commercial)</b>. Lessons 1–5 use this path.</p></div><div><b>Webex FedRAMP</b><p>Choose <b>FedRAMP — US Government cloud</b>. Follow the cloud lessons in the approved government environment.</p></div><div><b>On-Premise</b><p>Choose <b>On-Premise</b> for local device access. GET Config is cloud-only, and targeting uses IP addresses instead of Webex Device IDs.</p></div></div><div class="route"><span>Deployment</span><b>›</b><span>Organizations</span><b>›</b><span>Add Organization</span></div><ol class="compact-steps"><li>Select the appropriate deployment type.</li><li>For Cloud or FedRAMP, select <b>Connect with Webex</b> and complete browser sign-in with an account that can access the organization.</li><li>Return to CE-Deploy, wait for <b>Connected!</b>, then select <b>Done</b>.</li></ol><div class="checkpoint"><b>Checkpoint:</b> the organization name appears in the upper-right organization switcher.</div><div class="note"><b>Normal setup uses Webex sign-in.</b> “Have a developer token?” is for short tests. Developer tokens expire after 12 hours and cannot refresh automatically.</div></article>
  </div></div></section>

  <section id="foundation" class="band-section"><div class="container"><header class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Foundation · 10 minutes</p><h2>Know what CE-Deploy is about to act on.</h2><p>The core workflow is: choose an organization, prepare an operation, resolve targets, inspect Pre-flight, run, and verify.</p></header><div class="concept-grid"><article><b>Organization</b><p>A saved connection to a Webex customer environment or local device environment. It determines which devices CE-Deploy can see.</p></article><article><b>Device and Device ID</b><p>A device is a RoomOS endpoint. A cloud Device ID is its long Webex identifier; use Browse to select by familiar name.</p></article><article><b>Workspace and location</b><p>A workspace is the Control Hub place assigned to a device. A location groups services and resources; it is not a tag.</p></article><article><b>Tag</b><p>A device label. Multiple comma-separated tags resolve devices containing all supplied tags.</p></article><article><b>xAPI</b><p>The RoomOS interface for commands, status, and configuration. Commands may change behavior; GET Config reads configuration.</p></article><article><b>Pre-flight</b><p>The review gate that resolves targets and checks readiness. It is not proof that the final operation succeeded.</p></article></div><div class="flow-line"><span>Active organization</span><b>→</b><span>Operation</span><b>→</b><span>Targets</span><b>→</b><span>Pre-flight</span><b>→</b><span>Run</span><b>→</b><span>Verify</span></div><div class="completion"><b>Foundation check</b><span>You can explain which organization is active, whether an action reads or changes a device, and why the Pre-flight count must match your intent.</span></div>
    <details class="knowledge"><summary>Knowledge check: organization and Pre-flight</summary><ol><li>What determines which devices CE-Deploy can see?</li><li>Pre-flight resolves three devices when you expected one. What is the next action?</li></ol><p><b>Answers:</b> The active organization and target criteria determine visibility. Cancel and correct the organization, target, filter, tags, or CSV; never continue with an unexpected count.</p></details>
    <button class="complete-toggle" type="button" data-course-id="foundation">Mark Foundation complete</button>
  </div></section>

  <section id="first-session" class="first-session"><div class="container"><header class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Recommended first session</p><h2>Stop after your first successful target check.</h2><p>A new administrator does not need to complete the advanced course in one sitting.</p></header><ol class="session-path"><li>Install CE-Deploy and verify <b>Settings → About</b>.</li><li>Connect one organization and confirm it in the upper-right switcher.</li><li>Complete Foundation and the interface orientation below.</li><li>Complete Lesson 1 with one pilot device.</li><li>Stop. Continue into xAPI only after an approved change window is available.</li></ol>
  <h3>Interface orientation</h3><div class="orientation-grid"><div><b>Main navigation</b><span>Switches between Deployment, Settings, and other application areas.</span></div><div><b>Deployment sidebar</b><span>Opens Organizations, Apps, Tools, xAPI, and Customizations.</span></div><div><b>Organization switcher</b><span>Upper-right control that sets the active device environment.</span></div><div><b>Operation tabs</b><span>Choose Commands, CSV, Template, or cloud-only GET Config.</span></div><div><b>Deployment Options</b><span>Choose Device ID, tags, or another supported target.</span></div><div><b>Pre-flight</b><span>Review resolved devices, compatibility, and blocking issues.</span></div><div><b>Deployment summary</b><span>Shows the per-device outcome after execution.</span></div><div><b>Tools and files</b><span>Use Tools for exports, tests, and downloaded reports.</span></div></div>
  <div class="permissions"><h3>Webex account and permissions</h3><p>Use the Webex account authorized to manage the intended organization. Select Commercial or FedRAMP before sign-in. If the organization does not appear, confirm the account, its Control Hub access, and the selected cloud. Do not compensate by pasting another person’s token.</p></div></div></section>

  <section id="lessons" class="course-index"><div class="container"><header class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Guided lessons</p><h2>Build confidence on one device first.</h2></header><div class="lesson-grid">{lessons.map(([n,t,badge,m,h]) => <a class="lesson-card" href={h}><span>{n}</span><i class:list={["track-badge",badge.toLowerCase()]}>{badge}</i><h3>{t}</h3><p>{m}</p><b>Open lesson →</b></a>)}</div></div></section>

  <div class="container training-detail">
    <article class="lesson" id="lesson-1"><LessonHeader number="01" eyebrow="Beginner · 15 minutes · No device change" title="Resolve one target, then cancel." objective="Select one named cloud device, verify it in Pre-flight, and exit without running an operation."/><LessonMeta prerequisites="Connected cloud organization; one online lab device" materials="Pilot device name" result="Exactly one intended device in Pre-flight"/><div class="route"><span>Deployment</span><b>›</b><span>xAPI</span><b>›</b><span>GET Config</span><b>›</b><span>Next: Select Devices</span></div><ol class="guided-steps"><li><div><strong>Activate the pilot organization.</strong><p>Use the upper-right organization switcher. If it is missing, select <b>Deployment → Organizations</b> and add it.</p><em>Checkpoint: the expected organization name is visible.</em></div></li><li><div><strong>Prepare a read-only request.</strong><p>Select <b>Deployment → xAPI → GET Config</b>. Turn on <b>Download Device Configurations</b>. Leave <b>xConfig Filter</b> empty.</p><em>Checkpoint: the page heading is “Download Device Configuration.”</em></div></li><li><div><strong>Select by familiar name.</strong><p>Select <b>Next: Select Devices</b>. Set <b>Deployment Target</b> to <b>Device ID</b>, select <b>Browse</b>, choose the pilot by name, then select <b>Select</b>.</p><em>Checkpoint: the display name beneath Device ID is your pilot.</em></div></li><li><div><strong>Resolve and exit.</strong><p>Select <b>Pre-flight</b>. Confirm exactly one intended device, then select <b>Cancel</b> and close Deployment Options.</p><em>Completed when: you can state the active organization, targeting method, device name, and resolved count.</em></div></li></ol><details><summary>Practice: compare Device ID and tag targeting</summary><p>Repeat target selection using <b>Tags</b>. Two comma-separated tags resolve devices containing both. Stop if the count is larger than expected.</p></details><details class="knowledge"><summary>Knowledge check</summary><p><b>Scenario:</b> You expected one pilot but Pre-flight lists three. What do you do?</p><p><b>Answer:</b> Cancel. Confirm the active organization and targeting inputs, then run Pre-flight again. The correct count is a required safety condition.</p></details><button class="complete-toggle" type="button" data-course-id="lesson-1">Mark Lesson 1 complete</button><div class="lesson-footer"><a href="#foundation">← Foundation</a><a href="#lesson-2">Lesson 2 →</a></div></article>

    <article class="lesson" id="lesson-2"><LessonHeader number="02" eyebrow="Intermediate · 20 minutes · Controlled change" title="Run one explicit xAPI operation and capture its output." objective="Set a pilot device to a known volume, capture the response, and restore the original volume."/><LessonMeta prerequisites="Lesson 1; approval to change pilot volume" materials="Current pilot volume; quiet test period" result="Successful output row and restored original volume"/><div class="callout danger"><b>This operation changes the pilot.</b> Confirm the room is unoccupied and the device is not in a call. On the device touch controller, open the volume control and record the displayed current volume before continuing. Restore it at the end.</div><div class="definition-row"><div><b>Current runtime volume</b><span>The live level shown on the device controller.</span></div><div><b>Default configured volume</b><span>The startup/default configuration, which may differ from the live level.</span></div><div><b>Command response</b><span>Proof the command was accepted; verify the visible device state separately.</span></div></div><div class="route"><span>Deployment</span><b>›</b><span>xAPI</span><b>›</b><span>Commands</span><b>›</b><span>Add Command</span></div><ol class="guided-steps"><li><div><strong>Add the controlled training command.</strong><p>Select <b>Add Command</b>, then <b>Search commands</b>. Find <code>Audio Volume Set</code> and set <b>Level</b> to <code>50</code>. Commands can change state; they are not read-only status queries.</p><em>Checkpoint: the card shows the intended command and no red validation indicator.</em></div></li><li><div><strong>Choose one pilot and request output.</strong><p>Select <b>Next: Select Devices</b>, browse to the pilot by Device ID, and turn on <b>Collect Command Outputs</b>. Run <b>Pre-flight</b>.</p><em>Stop if the preview differs or more than one device resolves.</em></div></li><li><div><strong>Run and read the report.</strong><p>Continue from Pre-flight. In the deployment summary, open <b>Command Output Report</b>. Confirm Device, Command, Status Code, Status, and Result fields.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Verify the visible result.</strong><p>The device controller should show volume 50. If it does not, stop and compare the output report with device availability.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Restore and prove the original value.</strong><p>Repeat the operation with the recorded starting volume, keep <b>Collect Command Outputs</b> enabled, and open the second report. Confirm success and verify the controller shows the original value.</p><em>Completed when: both reports exist and the pilot is visibly restored.</em></div></li></ol><details><summary>Instructor note: why this operation?</summary><p>Volume provides an immediate, reversible result that can be checked without installing content. Use only an unoccupied lab room, never an active meeting space.</p></details><details><summary>Interpret common outcomes</summary><ul><li><b>Success:</b> device accepted the command; verify actual state.</li><li><b>Offline or timeout:</b> confirm availability before retrying.</li><li><b>Unsupported:</b> check model and RoomOS compatibility.</li><li><b>Output missing:</b> enable Collect Command Outputs before execution.</li></ul></details><details class="knowledge"><summary>Knowledge check</summary><p><b>Scenario:</b> The report says Success but the visible volume did not change. Is the task complete?</p><p><b>Answer:</b> No. A successful API response is delivery evidence. The desired device state must also be verified, followed by restoration.</p></details><button class="complete-toggle" type="button" data-course-id="lesson-2">Mark Lesson 2 complete</button><div class="lesson-footer"><a href="#lesson-1">← Lesson 1</a><a href="#lesson-3">Lesson 3 →</a></div></article>

    <article class="lesson" id="lesson-3"><LessonHeader number="03" eyebrow="Intermediate · 25 minutes · Controlled change" title="Build, validate, and repair an xAPI CSV." objective="Export identifiers, prepare a two-device CSV, identify a delimiter fault, and verify row-to-target mapping."/><LessonMeta prerequisites="Lesson 2; two approved pilot devices" materials="Working and broken practice files" result="Pre-flight count equals valid pilot rows"/><div class="downloads"><a class="btn primary" href={`${base}training/xapi-pilot-template.csv`} download>Working template</a><a class="btn" href={`${base}training/xapi-broken-practice.csv`} download>Broken practice file</a><a class="btn" href={`${base}training/xapi-restoration-template.csv`} download>Restoration template</a></div><h3>CSV columns</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>C</th><th>D–M</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Device ID</td><td>Reserved — empty</td><td>Reserved — empty</td><td>Commands 1–10</td></tr></tbody></table></div><pre>Device ID,,,Command 1
REPLACE_WITH_DEVICE_ID,,,xCommand Audio Volume Set Level: 50</pre><div class="route"><span>Deployment</span><b>›</b><span>Tools</span><b>›</b><span>Export</span><b>›</b><span>Device IDs</span></div><ol class="guided-steps"><li><div><strong>Export current identifiers.</strong><p>Select <b>Tools → Export</b>, choose <b>Device IDs</b>, and use the narrowest filter. Preserve the export unchanged.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Stage A: build one pilot row.</strong><p>Replace only the first sample ID and remove the second data row. Column A is Device ID, B and C remain empty, and commands occupy D–M. Do not execute the file in Stage A.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Inspect as plain text.</strong><p>Confirm comma delimiters, UTF-8, one device per row, and two empty fields between Device ID and command. Compare with the broken semicolon file.</p><em>Checkpoint: each row looks like <code>device-id,,,xCommand…</code>.</em></div></li><li><div><strong>Stage A: upload, Pre-flight, and cancel.</strong><p>Select <b>xAPI → CSV</b>, enable <b>Enable CSV-based commands</b>, upload the file, select <b>Next: Select Devices</b>, and run <b>Pre-flight</b>. Confirm one device and the intended command, then cancel.</p><em>Required completion: you repaired the delimiter problem and proved one correct row without executing it.</em></div></li><li><div><strong>Stage B: optional instructor-approved execution.</strong><p>Only with approval, add a second pilot row, record both live volumes, Pre-flight again, execute, and use the restoration template to return both devices to their original values.</p><em>Stop if either resolved count, command, or original value is uncertain.</em></div></li></ol><details><summary>Diagnose six common CSV faults</summary><ul><li><b>Semicolon delimiter:</b> fields do not split into columns.</li><li><b>Missing empty columns:</b> command shifts before column D.</li><li><b>Duplicate Device ID:</b> the same target appears twice.</li><li><b>Blank Device ID:</b> a row has no valid target.</li><li><b>Unsupported command:</b> compatibility or validation blocks the row.</li><li><b>Modified identifier:</b> spreadsheet formatting changed the Device ID.</li></ul></details><details class="knowledge"><summary>Knowledge check</summary><p><b>Scenario:</b> Your file has two rows but Pre-flight resolves one device. Should you execute?</p><p><b>Answer:</b> No. Check blank or duplicate IDs, delimiter, and organization before continuing.</p></details><button class="complete-toggle" type="button" data-course-id="lesson-3">Mark Lesson 3 complete</button><div class="lesson-footer"><a href="#lesson-2">← Lesson 2</a><a href="#lesson-4">Lesson 4 →</a></div></article>

    <article class="lesson" id="lesson-4"><LessonHeader number="04" eyebrow="Advanced · 30 minutes · Conditional" title="Configure CE-Deploy for a TLS inspection proxy." objective="Identify the proxy mode, add the correct trust certificate, and prove Webex connectivity without disabling verification."/><LessonMeta prerequisites="Network-team details; current CE-Deploy release" materials="Proxy host/port; public CA; credentials if required" result="Diagnostics and organization connection both pass"/><div class="callout skip"><b>Skip this lesson unless your network uses a proxy or TLS inspection.</b> Continue to Lesson 5 or Troubleshooting if direct Webex connectivity already works.</div><h3>Obtain these answers first</h3><ul class="prereq-list"><li>Proxy type: OS-managed, explicit, or PAC</li><li>Proxy host and port</li><li>Whether authentication is required</li><li>The required root or issuing CA</li><li>Whether mutual TLS is required</li></ul><div class="concept-grid compact"><article><b>Root CA</b><p>The organization’s top trust anchor.</p></article><article><b>Issuing CA</b><p>The CA that may sign the proxy certificate.</p></article><article><b>TLS inspection</b><p>The proxy presents a replacement certificate.</p></article><article><b>Mutual TLS</b><p>The proxy also requires a client certificate and key.</p></article></div><div class="route"><span>Settings</span><b>›</b><span>Network &amp; Proxy</span><b>›</b><span>Web Proxy</span></div><div class="decision"><b>Use the path supplied by the network team.</b><p><b>OS-managed:</b> Auto Web Proxy Detection. <b>Explicit or PAC:</b> Custom Web Proxy. <b>TLS inspection:</b> add the public CA. <b>Mutual TLS:</b> add client certificate and key only when required.</p></div><ol class="guided-steps"><li><div><strong>Record current settings.</strong><p>Record enabled sliders, proxy address, port, and certificate filenames. Do not record passwords or private keys.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Configure one proxy method.</strong><p>Enable <b>Auto Web Proxy Detection</b> or <b>Custom Web Proxy</b>. Custom overrides auto detection.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Keep validation enabled and add trust.</strong><p>Enable <b>Verify TLS Certificates</b>. Under <b>Certificate-Based Proxy (optional)</b>, browse for <b>CA Certificate (.pem, .crt)</b>. The picker also accepts <code>.cer</code>.</p><em>Checkpoint: client certificate and key remain empty unless mutual TLS is required.</em></div></li><li><div><strong>Save and test.</strong><p>Select <b>Save Web Proxy</b>. Then run <b>Deployment → Tools → Tests → Webex Diagnostics → Run Diagnostics</b>. Under <b>Organizations</b>, open the active organization menu and select <b>Test Connection</b>.</p><em>Completed when: both tests pass without a certificate error.</em></div></li></ol><h3>Symptom map</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely layer</th><th>First check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Name cannot resolve</td><td>DNS</td><td>Resolver and proxy hostname</td></tr><tr><td>Connection times out</td><td>TCP/firewall</td><td>Route, port, Webex destinations</td></tr><tr><td>HTTP 407</td><td>Proxy authentication</td><td>Username, password, policy</td></tr><tr><td>Unknown CA</td><td>TLS trust</td><td>Presented chain and supplied CA</td></tr><tr><td>Sign-in rejected</td><td>Webex authentication</td><td>Cloud type and account</td></tr><tr><td>Wrong or missing org</td><td>Organization authorization</td><td>Control Hub permissions</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details><summary>Failure decision tree and rollback</summary><ul><li><b>DNS:</b> verify resolver and proxy host.</li><li><b>407:</b> verify proxy authentication.</li><li><b>CA error:</b> verify the CA signing the certificate presented on this workstation; ask the network team about bundle order.</li><li><b>Timeout:</b> verify firewall and required Webex destinations.</li><li><b>Certificate bundles:</b> the network team must specify bundle contents and order.</li><li><b>Rollback:</b> restore each recorded slider, proxy address, port, and certificate field; select Save Web Proxy; rerun Webex Diagnostics and Test Connection.</li></ul></details><div class="callout danger"><b>Do not permanently disable Verify TLS Certificates.</b> Repair the trust chain instead.</div><details class="knowledge"><summary>Knowledge check</summary><p><b>Scenario:</b> DNS succeeds and the proxy returns an unknown-CA error. Which layer should you investigate?</p><p><b>Answer:</b> TLS trust. Confirm the CA that signs the certificate presented to this workstation.</p></details><button class="complete-toggle" type="button" data-course-id="lesson-4">Mark Lesson 4 complete</button><div class="lesson-footer"><a href="#lesson-3">← Lesson 3</a><a href="#lesson-5">Lesson 5 →</a></div></article>

    <article class="lesson" id="lesson-5"><LessonHeader number="05" eyebrow="Intermediate · 25 minutes · Reversible change" title="Deploy, verify, and remove one training macro." objective="Deploy a harmless logging macro to one pilot, verify its runtime output, then remove it."/><LessonMeta prerequisites="Pilot with macros enabled; approval to deploy test code" materials="Training macro below" result="Start message observed, then macro removed"/><div class="downloads"><a class="btn primary" href={`${base}training/ce-deploy-training-macro.js`} download>Download training macro</a></div><div class="route"><span>Deployment</span><b>›</b><span>Customizations</span><b>›</b><span>Macros</span><b>›</b><span>Deploy Macro</span></div><ol class="guided-steps"><li><div><strong>Upload and review.</strong><p>Under <b>Macro JavaScript File</b>, upload the training macro. Open <b>Code Preview</b> and review <b>Issues</b>.</p><em>Checkpoint: it only logs a start message and reads active-call count.</em></div></li><li><div><strong>Configure the pilot.</strong><p>Name it <code>CE-Deploy Training</code>, enable <b>Activate on deployment</b>, and leave <b>Skip Transpilation</b> off.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Pre-flight and deploy.</strong><p>Select <b>Next: Select Devices</b>, browse to one pilot, and run <b>Pre-flight</b>. Continue only when file, name, activation state, and device are correct.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Confirm the runtime is available.</strong><p>Select <b>Deployment → Apps → Macro Factory</b>. Select the pilot device card to open its detail panel. Under engine status, confirm the runtime is running. If it is not, stop and enable Macros Mode through the device’s approved administration workflow.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Verify runtime behavior.</strong><p>In the device detail panel, inspect <b>Engine Logs</b> for <code>CE-Deploy training macro started</code> and <code>CE-Deploy training: active calls = 0</code>. Zero is a valid successful result when the room has no active call.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Deactivate, remove, and refresh.</strong><p>In <b>Deployment → Apps → Macro Factory</b>, expand the pilot device, find <b>CE-Deploy Training</b>, use the pause control titled <b>Deactivate</b>, then the trash control titled <b>Remove</b>. Confirm <b>Remove Macros</b>, refresh the inventory, and confirm the macro is absent.</p><em>Completed when: the expected logs were observed and a refreshed inventory no longer lists the macro.</em></div></li></ol><details><summary>Diagnose the correct stage</summary><p><b>Upload:</b> file access. <b>Review:</b> code validation. <b>Deployment:</b> target or API. <b>Runtime:</b> macro logic, macros mode, or device state.</p></details><details class="knowledge"><summary>Knowledge check</summary><p><b>Scenario:</b> Deployment succeeded, but no training log appears. Where do you investigate?</p><p><b>Answer:</b> Check Macro Factory engine status, Macros Mode, activation state, and runtime logs.</p></details><button class="complete-toggle" type="button" data-course-id="lesson-5">Mark Lesson 5 complete</button><div class="lesson-footer"><a href="#lesson-4">← Lesson 4</a><a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting →</a></div></article>
  </div>

  <section id="recovery"><div class="container"><header class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Authentication and organization recovery</p><h2>Test the saved connection before rebuilding it.</h2></header><ol class="guided-steps"><li><div><strong>Confirm the active organization.</strong><p>Read the upper-right switcher. Select the intended organization if another is active.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Test the saved connection.</strong><p>Select <b>Deployment → Organizations</b>, open the organization card’s three-dot action menu, and select <b>Test Connection</b>.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Classify the result.</strong><p>A sign-in or expired-token response is authentication. A successful sign-in with a missing organization is usually account authorization or the wrong Commercial/FedRAMP cloud. A developer token older than 12 hours should be treated as expired.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Reconnect safely.</strong><p>If OAuth access cannot be refreshed, add the organization again through <b>Add Organization → Cloud or FedRAMP → Connect with Webex</b>, verify the new connection, and remove stale entries only after the replacement works.</p></div></li></ol><button class="complete-toggle" type="button" data-course-id="recovery">Mark Recovery complete</button></div></section>

  <section id="on-prem-path" class="band-section"><div class="container"><header class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Separate on-premises path</p><h2>Translate the workflow without cloud-only controls.</h2></header><p>Choose <b>On-Premise</b> when adding the organization. Target local devices by IP address or supported inventory selection. Commands and CSV remain available, but cloud-only <b>GET Config</b>, Webex Device IDs, and Control Hub tag behavior do not apply. Begin with connectivity and credentials for one lab endpoint before using the changing exercises.</p></div></section>

  <section id="operations-guide"><div class="container"><header class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Operational reference</p><h2>Know where results and evidence go.</h2></header><div class="triage-grid"><article><b>Downloaded reports</b><p>Open the deployment summary and use the available report action. Use Tools for exports and downloaded files appropriate to the workflow.</p></article><article><b>Deployment summaries</b><p>Compare attempted, successful, failed, and blocked devices. Delivery success still requires state verification.</p></article><article><b>Application updates</b><p>Compare <b>Settings → About</b> with the latest release page before troubleshooting a fixed issue.</p></article><article><b>Offline devices</b><p>Do not treat repeated retries as diagnosis. Confirm reachability, registration, model, RoomOS, and organization.</p></article><article><b>Compatibility</b><p>Read validation and Pre-flight messages before execution. Unsupported commands require a different operation or device version.</p></article><article><b>Support evidence</b><p>Collect time, version, OS, organization type, operation, target count, exact error, and sanitized logs.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section id="troubleshooting" class="band-section"><div class="container"><header class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Troubleshooting</p><h2>Find the failing layer before repeating an action.</h2></header><div class="triage-grid"><article><b>Cannot sign in</b><p>Confirm Commercial versus FedRAMP, browser completion, account access, and proxy connectivity. Reconnect the organization. A developer token may have expired.</p></article><article><b>Device not found</b><p>Confirm active organization, online state, exact tag spelling, and Device ID versus IP workflow.</p></article><article><b>Pre-flight differs</b><p>Stop. Compare organization, target, tags, filters, CSV rows, and duplicates.</p></article><article><b>Deployment failed</b><p>Separate transport, authorization, compatibility, and device rejection. Read the summary before retrying.</p></article><article><b>Need support</b><p>Record version, OS, organization type, operation, target, time, count, and exact error. Use <b>Settings → About → Open Logs Folder</b>.</p></article><article><b>Protect secrets</b><p>Remove tokens, passwords, private keys, passphrases, and sensitive identifiers before sharing.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section id="glossary"><div class="container"><header class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Glossary</p><h2>Terms used throughout the course.</h2></header><div class="glossary-grid"><div><b>Cloud organization</b><p>A Control Hub environment accessed through Webex APIs.</p></div><div><b>On-premises organization</b><p>An environment that connects directly to local devices.</p></div><div><b>Deployment summary</b><p>Final operation results; delivery still requires outcome verification.</p></div><div><b>Output report</b><p>Per-device response details when output collection is enabled.</p></div><div><b>PEM</b><p>Text encoding for certificates and keys with BEGIN and END markers.</p></div><div><b>Rollback</b><p>Steps or values used to return a pilot to its previous state.</p></div></div></div></section>

  <section id="capstone" class="capstone"><div class="container"><header class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Final assessment · 30 minutes</p><h2>Complete the workflow without the guided steps.</h2></header><ol class="capstone-list"><li>Confirm installed version and active organization.</li><li>Resolve one pilot and prove the Pre-flight count.</li><li>Run an approved operation, collect output, and restore the original value.</li><li>Repair the broken CSV and explain the fault.</li><li>Choose the first diagnostic for authentication, proxy, and offline-device symptoms.</li><li>Prepare a useful support note containing no secrets.</li></ol><div class="completion"><b>Course completion</b><span>You can select intended targets, predict the change, interpret the result, restore the pilot, and collect safe evidence.</span></div><h3>Passing rubric</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Pass evidence</th><th>Critical safety failure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Targeting</td><td>Organization, name, and count match</td><td>Continuing with unexpected devices</td></tr><tr><td>Change control</td><td>Original value recorded and restored</td><td>No rollback value or occupied room</td></tr><tr><td>CSV</td><td>Fault explained; Stage A Pre-flight matches</td><td>Executing an unverified file</td></tr><tr><td>Troubleshooting</td><td>Correct failing layer and first check</td><td>Disabling TLS verification as the fix</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Useful sanitized support note</td><td>Sharing tokens, passwords, or keys</td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="knowledge"><summary>Self-study answer key and instructor review</summary><p>Pass when every required targeting check is correct, all changing operations are restored, CSV Stage A is explained accurately, and no secrets are collected. Instructors should review the learner’s Pre-flight evidence, two command reports, restoration evidence, CSV diagnosis, and sanitized support note.</p></details><a class="btn primary top-link" href="#start-here">Review from the beginning ↑</a></div></section>
<script is:inline>
  (() => {
    const key = 'ce-deploy-training-progress-v1';
    const buttons = [...document.querySelectorAll('.complete-toggle')];
    const read = () => { try { return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(key) || '[]'); } catch { return []; } };
    const render = () => {
      const done = read();
      buttons.forEach((button) => { const active = done.includes(button.dataset.courseId); button.classList.toggle('done', active); button.textContent = active ? 'Completed ✓' : `Mark ${button.dataset.courseId.replace('lesson-', 'Lesson ')} complete`; });
      const count = document.getElementById('progressCount'); if (count) count.textContent = `${done.length} of ${buttons.length}`;
    };
    buttons.forEach((button) => button.addEventListener('click', () => { const id = button.dataset.courseId; const done = read(); localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(done.includes(id) ? done.filter((x) => x !== id) : [...done, id])); render(); }));
    document.getElementById('resetProgress')?.addEventListener('click', () => { localStorage.removeItem(key); render(); });
    render();
  })();
</script>
</BaseLayout>

